All:  Another "read all of this" blurt.

Updating this old Blurt seems to be necessary.

To be clear:  UNDER NO CIRCUMSTANCES are you to represent that 1 DR step is=
 ok in a butt fusion.

Forget Isco, CMF, PPI, or any other PE "expert".  They are all wrong.  You =
are not to make Harco culpable with such advice.  This is not a suggestion.=
  It is strict direction.

You may allow that there is a "word of mouth" convention that one DR step i=
s acceptable.  Harco recommends that matching wall thickness is best practi=
ce and that fittings with machined ends are available to match the thicknes=
s of the pipe to which it is fused.  The cost of such a measure is low comp=
ared to the cost of the system.  In the end we will ship what is ordered.  =
It is the user's responsibility to attend to this practice.  Any mismatch w=
ill reduce the life of the system an indeterminate amount from what it coul=
d be.

Understand that because of the stress concentration created by the mismatch=
 the life of the system is reduced an indeterminate amount from what it cou=
ld be.  The bigger the mismatch the more the life reduction.  We have alrea=
dy seen in golf (the Polygate affair) that 11 to 17 and 11 to 15.5 leads to=
 failures within a few years.  It remains to be seen how 11 to 13.5 will do=
.

In the Gas application and Municipal Water application mismatches are not p=
ermitted.  At the Golf Course Builders two years ago in Louisville I visite=
d the Isco headquarters operation.  Every fitting on the yard and in the wa=
rehouses had machined ends.  In Europe butt fusing unlike DR's are not allo=
wed and unheard of.  If such a connection is required electrofusion couplin=
gs are used.

At the Fall meeting of the PPI I challenged Camille Rubiez (Director of one=
 of the PPI divisions) to support his assertion that one step was ok at the=
 Golf Course Builders in August.  He had denied suitability several years a=
go what was new?  He said it was on the PPI web site.  I challenged him to =
show me.  He came back chastened and asked me if I wanted to take out a tas=
k to study the matter.  I declined that honor.  I asked him not to repeat t=
hat nonsense in the golf market in the future.

One other insight:  It is to our commercial advantage that matching DR's an=
d machined fittings are adopted.  We are better positioned to do this than =
CMF.  It is also the right thing to do.  A win win.

J

From: John Riordan [mailto:JRiordan@harcofittings.com]
